        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="4" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house has a few key locatiions where excess air leakage (heat loss) that should be addressed. The current air leakage locations include:



** The range exhaust fan for the BBQ in the rear sunroom. The window beside the BBQ would not close tightly and also represented air leakage.



** This house used to have 2 sets of laundry, and now only has one set installed. The dryer vent for the second set is laying on the floor &amp; open to the underside of the house ...... representing a 4" circle of heat loss.



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The current boiler &amp; hot water tank are meant to be direct vent models, drawing their combustion air from outside, however both have their intake air openings set to draw air from inside the house. During the blower door test, I brought you to the mechanical room so you could feel the amount of air coming from each. On a windy day, that same amount of air is being sucked out of your house (especially from the boiler pipe).



** The 2 bathroom fans on the upper bedroom level allow alot of air leakage and should be replaced with new high efficiency Energy Star rated units, that use very little electricity, operate at low noise (sone) levels, and will have a good internal damper to seal &amp; prevent excess air leakage heat loss.



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 356 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 9.4 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

Most of the windows in your house have already been upgraded to triple pane units that are reasonably energy efficient abnd they seem to close &amp; seal quite well. You recvently upgraded a window in the lower left side of your house so that it was of the proper size &amp; opening to qualify as "egress" for the secondary suite you are currently developing in that section of the house.



The only windows that are not triple pane are the 4 large double pane windows in the rear heated sunroom. Three of them had a window film installed to help reduce air leakage and reduce the cold radiating from them. The one window (nearest the BBQ) did not have a window film, as you indicated it is regularly opened when the BBQ is operating in the house and the exhaust fan in the range hood above is operating. We identified during the blower door air leakage test that this window was not sealing well at all (even when closed) and allowed alot of air leakage heat loss.



Those 4 windows are large &amp; will be expensive to upgrade, and you may just want to keep doing what you are now (ensuring a good window film is installed each fall) and try your best to keep that one window shut tightly (have it adjusted and/or upgrade the weatherstripping).



I modeled the expected annual energy savings of those 4 large rear windows were upgraded to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified models. In addition, I recommend a full frame replacement window that is a triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing those 4 rear doublew pane sunroom windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 442 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 11.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system so please see the previous "Heating System" section of this report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an "up to 87%" efficient gas boiler that you indicated has had a few issues in the previous heating season. In addition, you have a domestic hot water tank that will operate at a much lower efficiency.



The mechanical room for the current boiler and hot water tank used to have a combustion aiir supply pipe (located at floor level behind the hot water tank) that would bring in outside combustion air for these 2 appliances to use - you will notice that pipe has been plugged). The current boiler &amp; hot water tank were designed to be "direct vent" to the outside, however their combustion air supply pipes have been "opened" into the basement so they still draw inside house air for combustion.



During the blower door test, I brought you to the mechanical room so you could feel the amount of air coming from each. On a windy day, that same amount of air is being sucked out of your house (especially from the boiler pipe). In addition, your house used to have 2 sets of laundry and there are 2 dryer vent openings down into the open space below the floor. One dryer is hooked up &amp; the other pipe is laying open ....... and essentially brings in combustion air for the boiler &amp; hot water tank.



In Inuvik, where the heating season is long, it is very common for homeowners to install a "combination" boiler that heats the house and can provide continuous on-demand domestic hot water heating at a very high combined efficiency.

For your longer term planning, I suggest you consider an upgrade to a 95% AFUE combination gas boiler that will both heat your house &amp; provide your hot water. This new boiler will direct vent out the rear wall of the house via 2 PVC pipes, and will eliminate the need for any "open" combustion air pipes in the mechanical room.



Savings: By replacing your current boiler &amp; hot water tank with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing "combi" gas boiler, you could potentially save $ 1,533 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 36.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has four toilets and the one on the upper floor (left side off of hallway) is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, 3 x bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.0 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. All of your current combustion appliances are either power vented (boiler &amp; hot water tank) or direct vent (gas fireplace &amp; gas fireplace/stove) and are not highly susceptible to back-drafting at lower pressure differences.



During my assessment of your home, there was both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood

        - Three bathroom fans

        - A clothes dryer

        - A large volume exhaust fan in the hood above the BBQ (in the rear sunroom)



As discussed, you had a leak around the ventilation fan in the main floor bathroom (now fixed) and we noticed quite a bit of aor leakage from the 2 bathroom fans on the upper bedroom level. You should consider upgrading those 2 bathroom fans to high efficiency Energy Star rated units, that use very little electricity, operate at low noise (sone) levels, and will have a good internal damper to seal &amp; prevent excess air leakage heat loss. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Inuvik



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.73 per kWh thereafter

Synthetic Natural Gas (Utilidor Piped) - $37.21/Gigajoule

Oil  - $1.46 per litre 

Propane - $0.69 per litre delivered

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $12.60 per bag, $630.00 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
